course: dictionary
lesson: 7701
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is right. «Grapes grow on a woody climbing vine that clings with curly tendrils and is pruned hard each winter.» A vine is a plant, not food, so pruned must mean cut back.
explain: Look at the clues. The vine clings, grows, and is pruned hard each winter. Pruning a plant means cutting off some branches so it grows better. Swap your meaning into the sentence: "is cut back hard each winter" still makes sense, so your meaning works.
explain: Prune can also mean a food. A prune is a dried plum, dark and wrinkly. The same sentence mentions raisins and sultanas, which are dried grapes, so the text helps you link the dried fruits together. Ask: which meaning fits this sentence?
explain: Prune can also mean to cut out parts you do not need, such as words in a story. When you meet prune in a book, read the words around it first. The clues will show you which meaning the writer wants.
fact: Prune can be a noun (a prune, prunes) or a verb (prune, prunes, pruned, pruning).
fact: A prune is a dried plum, dark and wrinkly, eaten as a snack or cooked.
fact: To prune a tree or bush means to cut off some branches so it grows better.
fact: To prune something means to cut out parts you do not need, such as words in a story.
fact: «Fingers like prunes» means fingers that look wrinkly, like a dried plum.
exercise: «Grapes grow on a woody climbing vine that is pruned hard each winter.» What does pruned mean here? | To tie up with string | *To cut off some branches so it grows better | To dry out in the sun | To eat as a snack
exercise: Nan puts prunes in her porridge. What is a prune? | A type of flower | A small green grape | A kind of knife for cutting hedges | *A dried plum, dark and wrinkly
exercise: Mrs Hale tells the class to prune their long sentences. What does she want them to do? | *Cut out the parts they do not need | Write them in pencil | Make the sentences longer | Read them aloud twice
test: In «The gardener will prune the roses in March», which word is the verb? | the roses | The gardener | *prune | March
test: «Her story was long and wandering, so she pruned it.» What does pruned most likely mean here? | Made it longer and more detailed | *Cut out the parts she did not need | Dried it in the sun | Painted it in bright colours
exam: What does the phrase «fingers like prunes» suggest? | Fingers that are very long and thin | *Fingers that look wrinkly, like a dried plum | Fingers that are cold and blue | Fingers covered in purple paint
